-- title:
--   Schönhage–Strassen: integer multiplication on multitape TMs in time O(n log n log log n)
-- statement:
--   There is a deterministic multitape Turing machine $\mathcal M$ (in the sense of `Def_IntMul_MultitapeModel`) multiplying integers in time $O(n\log n\log\log n)$:
--   $$\mathrm{MulTimeBound}\big(n\mapsto n\cdot\ln n\cdot\ln\ln n\big).$$
--   Explicitly: for every $n\ge1$ there is a bound $T$ such that on every input $x\#y$ with $x,y\in\{0,1\}^n$ the machine halts within $T$ steps with output $\mathrm{bin}_{2n}(\mathrm{val}\,x\cdot\mathrm{val}\,y)$, and there are $c>0$ and $n_0$ such that for all $n\ge \max(n_0,1)$ this holds with $T=c\,n\ln n\ln\ln n$.
--
--   The machine is `ssMachine`, the compilation of the item-language program `ssProg` (main program, `FM`, forward FFT, inverse FFT, powers of two). The proof bounds the cost of `FM` at size $N$ by the potential $\Phi(N)=2^{2100}N\,\ell\,\lambda$ with $\ell\approx\log N$, $\lambda\approx 4\log\ell$, by induction using `fmMain_runs` (one level costs $2^kC$ plus `fmLevelCost N`, and the parameter inequalities show that the potential absorbs this when $C=\Phi(N')$); the main program reads the inputs, calls `FM` at $N_0=2^{|n+1|_2+1}=\Theta(n)$ and writes the product; compiler correctness and `multipliesAt_of_exec` transfer the bound to the Turing machine.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** `Real.log` is the natural logarithm (the base only affects the constant), and for small $n$ where $\ln\ln n\le 0$ the bound is only required for $n\ge n_0$. The platform-facing alias is `IntMul.TM.schonhage_strassen`, with the same statement.
-- source:
--   A. Schönhage, V. Strassen, Schnelle Multiplikation großer Zahlen, Computing 7 (1971), 281–292 (main theorem: O(n log n log log n) multitape TM multiplication). Lemma `NLogN.ss_mulTimeBound` of the Lean development NLogN/SSFinal.lean (lines 66–83), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

